# Command titles and descriptions: `command-<id>` with the id's `.` as `-`,
# as kiln-command keys them (i18n design, section 4).

command-vim-move = Vim Move
    .description = Moves every caret by a vim motion, `count` times or to row `count`; the count is bounded by the text. In a visual mode it moves the vim cursor, keeping the anchor. A search motion's pattern becomes the last search; one that fails moves nothing and leaves a message, as does a mark motion whose mark isn't set. A jump sets the jump mark (`''`) to where it left from.
command-vim-operate = Vim Operate
    .description = Applies a vim operator (delete, yank, change, indent, outdent) to a motion or text object at every caret, filling the registers as vim does; one transaction for all carets. Change then starts an insert session in the same transaction. In a visual mode it acts on the selection, as do toggle_case, lowercase, uppercase, join and replace.
command-vim-put = Vim Put
    .description = Puts a register after (or before) every caret, `count` times: whole lines below or above the caret's row, a block from the primary caret's column. At most 64 MiB at once.
command-vim-insert = Vim Insert
    .description = Enters insert mode before or after the caret, at the row's first non-blank or end, or on a new row below or above: one transaction until normal mode, typed text going in `count` times.
command-vim-normal_mode = Vim Normal Mode
    .description = Leaves insert mode: repeats the typed text for the count, ends the session's transaction and steps the caret back one grapheme.
command-vim-resume_insert = Vim Resume Insert
    .description = Ends the normal-mode command insert mode's ctrl-o runs, resuming the insert or replace session it left at the carets: what `<Esc>`, or a key that fails, commits once nothing is pending.
command-vim-replace_mode = Vim Replace Mode
    .description = Enters replace mode at the caret: typed text overwrites the row and goes on past its end, backspace puts back what it overwrote; one transaction until normal mode, typed text going in `count` times.
command-vim-virtual_replace_mode = Vim Virtual Replace Mode
    .description = Enters virtual replace mode (`gR`) at the caret: like replace mode, but each typed character replaces the screen columns it takes, a tab as the spaces to its next stop; typed text overwrites the row and goes on past its end, backspace puts back what it overwrote; one transaction until normal mode, typed text going in `count` times.
command-vim-replace_normal_mode = Vim Normal Mode from Replace
    .description = Leaves replace mode: overwrites with the typed text again for the count, ends the session's transaction and steps the caret back one grapheme.
command-vim-join = Vim Join Lines
    .description = Joins `count` rows (at least two) from each caret's, dropping indentation and putting in one space where vim does.
command-vim-insert_last = Vim Insert At Last Insert
    .description = Enters insert mode where insert mode was last left (the `^` mark), as `gi` does; at the caret if there is none.
command-vim-replace_char = Vim Replace Character
    .description = Replaces `count` graphemes from each caret with a character, if that many remain on the row.
command-vim-toggle_case = Vim Toggle Case
    .description = Toggles the case of `count` graphemes from each caret, as nvim's `~` does, and moves past them.
command-vim-undo = Vim Undo
    .description = Undoes `count` changes, putting the carets back where they were before the last one undone.
command-vim-redo = Vim Redo
    .description = Redoes `count` undone changes, putting the carets back where they were before the last one.
command-vim-repeat = Vim Repeat
    .description = Repeats the last change and the text its insert session typed, as one transaction; a count replaces the change's own.
command-vim-repeat_substitute = Vim Repeat Substitute
    .description = Repeats the last substitute with its flags on the cursor's line, or `count` lines from it (`&`, as nvim's `:&&`): one transaction, which `.` doesn't repeat.
command-vim-substitute_answer = Vim Substitute Answer
    .description = Answers the `:s` with the `c` flag asking about a match, as nvim's prompt takes `answer`: `y` replaces it and asks about the next, `n` skips it, `a` replaces it and every one after it, `l` replaces it and ends, `q` ends. Every replacement is one undo step; once it ends the message is nvim's report. Fails if no `:s` is asking.
command-vim-substitute_all_lines = Vim Substitute All Lines
    .description = Repeats the last substitute with its flags on every line, with the last search pattern (`g&`, as nvim's `:%s//~/&`): one transaction, which `.` doesn't repeat.
command-vim-record = Vim Record Macro
    .description = Starts recording the keys typed, in every mode, into register `register` (`a` to `z`; `A` to `Z` append), until `q`: `vim.recording` is the register meanwhile.
command-vim-record_stop = Vim Stop Recording
    .description = Ends the recording, leaving the keys typed as the register's text, charwise.
command-vim-play = Vim Play Macro
    .description = Plays register `register` (`a` to `z`, `@` for the last one played, or `Q` for the last one recorded) `count` times: its text, as keys, is left for the host to feed (`VimState::take_replay`). `:` runs the last ex line submitted (E30 if none) `count` times instead, stopping at the first failure. A replay stops at the first key that fails, or before it would feed more than 1,000,000 keys.
command-vim-set_mark = Vim Set Mark
    .description = Sets mark `name` (`a` to `z`, or `'` or `` ` `` for the jump mark) at the primary caret, or at the vim cursor in a visual mode.
command-vim-visual = Vim Visual Mode
    .description = Enters charwise, linewise or blockwise visual mode at the primary caret, switches to that kind from another, or leaves it if it's the current one.
command-vim-visual_exit = Vim Leave Visual Mode
    .description = Leaves visual mode, keeping its extent for `gv`, `'<` and `'>`.
command-vim-visual_swap = Vim Swap Selection Ends
    .description = Moves the visual cursor to the other end of the selection; in a block with `same_row`, to the other corner on its row.
command-vim-visual_put = Vim Put Over Selection
    .description = Replaces the visual selection with a register, `count` times: `p` (`before` unset) leaves what it replaced in the registers, `P` leaves them as they were.
command-vim-reselect = Vim Reselect
    .description = Selects the last visual extent again, in its kind; in visual mode the current extent becomes the last.
command-vim-select = Vim Select Text Object
    .description = Selects a text object in visual mode, or extends the selection by `count` more of it.
command-vim-command_line = Vim Command Line
    .description = Opens a command line of `kind` (`search_forward` for `/`, `search_backward` for `?`, `ex` for `:`): `vim.mode` becomes `cmdline` until the host submits or cancels the line it owns. A search line carries the pending count, register and operator to the submit. An ex line leaves a visual mode (setting `'<` and `'>`) and starts with `'<,'>` from one, or `.,.+{"{"}count-1{"}"}` after a count: `VimState::command_line_prefill`.
command-vim-command_line_submit = Vim Submit Command Line
    .description = Ends the command line with its `text`, back in the mode it came from, and runs it: a search moves to the `count`th match of the pattern (an empty one is the last search's), or applies the pending operator up to it; an ex line runs its command over its range (`:{"{"}range{"}"}`, `:d`, `:noh`, `:s`, `:&`). A line that fails leaves a message and changes nothing else.
command-vim-command_line_cancel = Vim Cancel Command Line
    .description = Ends the command line without running it, back in the mode it came from (a visual selection as it was), dropping a pending operator. Its `text`, if any, goes into the history, as nvim keeps a cancelled line.
command-vim-ex_line = Vim Run Ex Line
    .description = Runs `text` as an ex line, as a remap's `<Cmd>…<CR>` does: no command line opens or closes, and none goes into the history, so the mode and a visual selection stay as they are unless the command changes them. A line that fails leaves a message.
command-vim-normal_resume = Vim Resume Normal
    .description = Resumes the `:normal` that stopped at a map's `<Cmd>fe:…<CR>` command, now run: its remaining keys, then its remaining range rows. Does nothing if none waits.
command-vim-expand_selection = Vim Expand Selection
    .description = Grows the selection to the syntax node enclosing it and enters charwise visual mode over it; from a caret, the node at the caret. Does nothing if no larger node is known.
command-vim-shrink_selection = Vim Shrink Selection
    .description = Steps back to the selection before the last expand, if nothing has changed since; the caret the first expand started from is normal mode again.
command-vim-increment = Vim Increment
    .description = Adds `count` to (or with a negative `delta` takes it from) the first number at or after each caret on its row, or in a visual mode on each selected row, as nvim's CTRL-A and CTRL-X do.
command-vim-fold = Vim Fold
    .description = Opens or closes the editor's folds at the primary caret's row (`zo zc za zO zC zA`), every fold (`zR zM`) or those hiding the caret (`zv`), or deletes the fold at the caret (`zd`), every fold nested there (`zD`) or every fold (`zE`), which in Tenmoku opens them; `count` is the levels `zo`, `zc` and `za` act on. Candidates are the host's fold ranges, or indented blocks. Changes no text.
command-vim-scroll = Vim Scroll
    .description = Scrolls the view as vim's ctrl-d, ctrl-u, ctrl-e, ctrl-y, ctrl-f, ctrl-b, zt, zz, zb, z<CR>, z. and z- do, moving the cursor as they do (in a visual mode, the vim cursor). A count sets 'scroll' for ctrl-d and ctrl-u, is rows or pages for the others, and the row to scroll to for the z keys.
command-vim-jump = Vim Jump
    .description = Jumps to a match of two chars in the view, forward or backward, as gs and gS do without a count: straight to it if it's the only one, or to the nearest with safe labels on the rest (not after an operator), otherwise labelling them all and waiting for a label. One char is the flash style's: straight to its only match, otherwise labels on them all (with both, before and after the caret). With a count it's the counted jump over the whole text. With an operator it acts on the text up to the target.
command-vim-earlier = Vim Earlier Text State
    .description = Goes back `count` text states, as `g-` does: in the linear undo history, `count` undos, stopping at the oldest without failing.
command-vim-later = Vim Later Text State
    .description = Goes forward `count` text states, as `g+` does: in the linear undo history, `count` redos, stopping at the newest without failing.
command-vim-join_raw = Vim Join Lines Without Spaces
    .description = Joins `count` rows (at least two) from each caret's as `gJ` does: keeping their indentation, putting in no space and taking none out.
command-vim-jump_older = Vim Jump Older
    .description = Goes `count` entries back through the jumplist, as vim's ctrl-o does, to the entry's exact position. The first after a jump adds where it goes from and sets the jump mark there, so vim.jump_newer comes back. Past the oldest entry it fails with nothing changed.
command-vim-jump_newer = Vim Jump Newer
    .description = Goes `count` entries forward through the jumplist, as vim's ctrl-i and tab do, to the entry's exact position. Past the newest entry it fails with nothing changed.
command-vim-write_quit = Vim Write Quit
    .description = Runs `:x`, as ZZ does: the host saves a modified buffer and closes the window.
command-vim-quit_discard = Vim Quit Discard
    .description = Runs `:q!`, as ZQ does: the host closes the window, discarding changes.
command-vim-change = Vim Change
    .description = Goes to column 0 of the first row of the `count`th next (`forward`) or previous changed row against the editor's diff base, as vim's ]c and [c do. Fewer changes than `count` go as far as there are; none that way fails with nothing changed. A jump, setting the jump mark. In a visual mode it extends the selection.
command-vim-diff_get = Vim Diff Get
    .description = Runs `:diffget` for the cursor's row, as `do` does: the host gets the change.
command-vim-diff_put = Vim Diff Put
    .description = Runs `:diffput` for the cursor's row, as `dp` does: the host puts the change.
command-vim-blank_lines = Vim Blank Lines
    .description = Puts `count` empty rows below each caret's row, or `above` it, as nvim's ]<Space> and [<Space> do, in one undo step; the caret stays on its text.
command-vim-move_rows = Vim Move Rows
    .description = Moves each caret's row (in a visual mode, the selection's rows) `count` rows down, or `up`, as vim-unimpaired's ]e and [e do, the carets, the selection and the rows' marks going with them; as far as they can go near the buffer's edge, failing with nothing changed when they can't move at all. One undo step.
command-vim-line_undo = Vim Line Undo
    .description = Undoes every latest change on the last changed line, as vim's U does; U again puts them back. Fails with nothing changed when there's no such line.
command-vim-change_older = Vim Change Older
    .description = Goes `count` entries back through the changelist, as vim's g; does, to the entry's exact position; a count past the oldest goes to the oldest. At the oldest, with no change made, or in a visual mode it fails with nothing changed.
command-vim-change_newer = Vim Change Newer
    .description = Goes `count` entries forward through the changelist, as vim's g, does, to the entry's exact position; a count past the newest goes to the newest. At the newest, with no change made, or in a visual mode it fails with nothing changed.
command-vim-surround = Vim Surround
    .description = Deletes the brackets, quotes or HTML tag around each caret, or with `new` replaces them, as vim-surround's ds and cs do; one transaction for all carets. Fails with nothing changed when no caret has such a pair around it.
command-vim-surround_add = Vim Surround Add
    .description = Puts a pair of brackets or quotes, or an HTML tag, around what a motion or text object covers from each caret, or around the visual selection, as vim-surround's ys, yS and visual S do; one transaction for all carets. Fails with nothing changed when the target covers nothing.
command-vim-exchange_clear = Vim Exchange Clear
    .description = Clears the region the exchange operator (cx) marked, as vim-exchange's cxc does, so the next cx marks anew.
command-vim-filter = Vim Filter
    .description = Asks the host to run `command` (a shell command, in parts; none for the last one, E34 with none) over the rows `target` covers at the caret with `count`, as `.` repeats `!{"{"}motion{"}"}`: leaves `HostCommand::Filter`, whose output `VimState::apply_filter` puts in their place. Only for the user's own keys: any other actor, or a `:g` or `:normal` running it, gets `Shell commands run only from your own keys` and no host command.
